docs/HUMAN_APPROVALS.md:3:Tally keeps account access, data capture, repository changes, and paid work behind clear choices. A successful `tally doctor` check proves a connection is reachable; it does not approve Entire capture, CFO table creation, or a worker run. `tally doctor` checks that a coding CLI is signed in; it does not probe model access.
docs/HUMAN_APPROVALS.md:22:- **cfo.ai Ari context:** answering Yes to `Use cfo.ai for budget, spend and Ari context?` adds or updates a Tally-managed guide on the selected scenario page and enables one operational report per attempt in organization-scoped cfo.ai memory. The guide is only updated when it has Tally's marker; other page blocks are left alone. Memory reports are shared with members of the selected workspace. They include task title/ID and issue link, repository, model/tier and configured rates, token usage when reported, estimated cost and its provenance, attempt ceiling/outcome/routing decision, budget snapshot, and PR/Entire/Neatlogs/cfo.ai links. Reports exclude issue bodies, source code and diffs, worker prompts and transcripts, raw trace contents, and credentials. Dry runs do not publish reports. The local ledger remains Tally's record; a refused memory write leaves budget/spend integration connected, reports the sanitized reason, and does not fail verified work.
docs/HUMAN_APPROVALS.md:27:- `tally doctor` checks configured connections and agent sign-in. It does not start workers, test a model request, trust Entire hooks, create CFO tables, or publish repository settings.
docs/HUMAN_APPROVALS.md:39:If init says the Entire hooks are not trusted, review them in your coding agent's hooks view even when the hook files were created and pushed. If you skip it, Tally intentionally keeps Entire off. A config flag or `doctor` result cannot replace that human decision.
